      "page_idx": 10,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.372,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "INTRODUCTION TO TRIGONOMETRY"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.863,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "123"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.139,
            0.901,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "As you know, for finding the trigonometric ratios, we need to know the lengths of the sides of the triangle. So, let us suppose that $\\mathrm { A B } = 2 a$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.203,
            0.158,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": "Then,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.428,
            0.19,
            0.595,
            0.231
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { B D } =   { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } \\mathrm { B C } = a .",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.242,
            0.141,
            0.258
          ],
          "content": "and"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.285,
            0.24,
            0.673,
            0.259
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{AD}^{2}=\\mathrm{AB}^{2}-\\mathrm{BD}^{2}=(2a)^{2}-(a)^{2}=3a^{2},",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.278,
            0.205,
            0.296
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.427,
            0.27,
            0.546,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": "AD =\\overset{\\cdot}{a\\sqrt{3}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.302,
            0.255,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "Now, we have :"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.313,
            0.747,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": "\\sin 30^{\\circ} = \\frac{BD}{AB} = \\frac{a}{2a} = \\frac{1}{2}, \\cos 30^{\\circ} = \\frac{AD}{AB} = \\frac{a\\sqrt{3}}{2a} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.359,
            0.508,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "\\tan 30^{\\circ} = \\frac{\\mathrm{BD}}{\\mathrm{AD}} = \\frac{a}{a\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.408,
            0.697,
            0.453
          ],
          "content": "Also, cosec $3 0 ^ { \\circ } = \\frac { 1 } { \\sin 3 0 ^ { \\circ } } = 2 ,$ sec $30^{\\circ} - \\frac{1}{\\cos 30^{\\circ}} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{3}}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.221,
            0.458,
            0.465,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "\\cot 30^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\tan 30^{\\circ}} = \\sqrt{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.507,
            0.189,
            0.527
          ],
          "content": "Similarly,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.517,
            0.775,
            0.563
          ],
          "content": "\\sin 60^{\\circ} = \\frac{AD}{AB} = \\frac{a\\sqrt{3}}{2a} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\cos 60^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{2}, \\tan 60^{\\circ} = \\sqrt{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.567,
            0.665,
            0.608
          ],
          "content": "cosec $6 0 ^ { \\circ } = ( \\frac { 2 } { \\sqrt { 3 } } )$ sec $6 0 ^ { \\circ } = 2$ and cot $6 0 ^ { \\circ } = { \\frac { 1 } { \\sqrt { 3 } } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.619,
            0.47,
            0.639
          ],
          "content": "Trigonometric Ratios of $\\mathbf { 0 } ^ { \\circ }$ and $\\mathbf { 9 0 ^ { \\circ } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.646,
            0.672,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": "Let us see what happens to the trigonometric ratios of angle A, if it is made smaller and smaller in the right triangle ABC (see Fig. 8.16), till it becomes zero. $\\mathrm { A s }   \\angle \\mathrm { A }$ gets smaller and smaller, the length of the side BC decreases.The point C gets closer to point B, and finally when $\\mathrm { \\angle A }$ becomes very close to $0 ^ { \\circ }$ , AC becomes almost the same as AB (see Fig. 8.17)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.722,
            0.656,
            0.839,
            0.744
          ],
